Undermining an employee's work performance can be subtle, but it is never acceptable. One way of undermining an employee's performance is to compare their lack of experience or qualifications to others in the same team. This comparison should not be done too explicitly, as it could undermine the employee's confidence and sense of worth. It may be better to provide constructive feedback on their performance, providing areas that could use improvement and suggesting potential solutions that would help elevate their work performance.

At times, employers may feel like their employees are not performing up to the expected standards; however, it is never right to directly express these feelings without offering guidance or resources for improvement. Instead of simply highlighting what the issue is and walking away - offer advice and solutions which will help solve the problem while building self-confidence in your employees. Constructive criticism with actionable items should always precede any form of disciplinary action.
